---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from '../../../i18n/utils';

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);

const keywords =
  'online speedometer, free online speedometer, online speedometer free, online speedometer test, online speedometer mph, hardspeedometer test, speedometer online train, speedometer test online, speedometer digital, gps speedometer online, my speed km/h, speedometer online, live speed test, live speedometer, zspeed, gps speedometer';

const title = 'Speedometer Units Explained: km/h, mph, m/s and Knots';
const description =
  'km/h, mph, m/s and knots explained: when to use each, how to convert in your head, and how to compare an online speedometer with a dashboard.';

const breadcrumbLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://instanten.com/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Online Speedometer', item: 'https://instanten.com/onlinespeedometer/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://instanten.com/onlinespeedometer/blog/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'Speedometer Units Explained: km/h, mph, m/s and Knots', item: 'https://instanten.com/onlinespeedometer/blog/speedometer-units-kmh-mph-knots/' },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  keywords={keywords}
  type="article"
  ogImage="/og-image.png"
  ogImageAlt={title}
  breadcrumbLd={breadcrumbLd}>
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-[48rem] px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
    <a
      href={translatePath('/onlinespeedometer/blog/')}
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-body hover:text-ink transition-colors mb-8">← Back to Speedometer Blog</a
    >
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-2">
      Speedometer Units Explained: km/h, mph, m/s and Knots
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono mb-8">November 7, 2026</p>

    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Every speed has four names. The same 100 km/h is 62 mph, 27.8 m/s and 54 knots, and picking the wrong one turns a perfectly good <strong>online speedometer</strong> reading into a number you cannot use. This guide is about choosing a unit, converting between them, and avoiding the two mistakes that make readings look wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">This guide takes you through the four units the tool supports, when each one is the right choice, how to convert mentally, and how to compare a <strong>speedometer online</strong> figure with a dashboard that speaks a different language. It uses the free <strong>live speedometer</strong> on this site, but the conversions apply anywhere — including apps such as <em>zspeed</em>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      The four units, and who uses them
    </h2>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead><tr class="bg-canvas-elevated"><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Unit</th><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Where it is standard</th><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">100 km/h equals</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">km/h</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">India, Europe, most of the world</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">100 km/h</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">mph</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">UK, US, Ireland</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">62.1 mph</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">m/s</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Physics, wind and ballistics</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">27.8 m/s</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">knots</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Ships, aircraft, sailing</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">54.0 kn</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">A <strong>speedometer digital</strong> readout is only useful if the number matches the unit on the signs around you. In a 50 km/h zone, an mph number is worse than no number at all — it looks plausible and is wrong by a factor of 1.6.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 1: Set the unit before you move
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">The unit button sits next to the readout. Choose it first, before you press Start, because every figure on the page follows that choice: current speed, maximum, average and the trip cards all convert together.
    </p>

    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>Driving in India or Europe: <strong>km/h</strong>.</li>
      <li>Driving in the UK, US or Ireland: <strong>mph</strong>.</li>
      <li>A physics or sports measurement: <strong>m/s</strong>.</li>
      <li>On water or in the air: <strong>knots</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Changing units mid-trip does not reset anything, so you can switch back and forth. What it will not do is remember the choice between visits on a shared device — a small price for not leaving a profile behind.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 2: Convert without a calculator
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Two approximations cover almost every real situation, and both are easy to do in your head.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">km/h to mph</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Halve it and add a tenth of the original. For 100 km/h: half is 50, a tenth is 10, so about 60 mph — the exact answer is 62.1. For 80 km/h: 40 + 8 = 48, exact 49.7. Close enough to read a sign at a glance.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">km/h to m/s</h3>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Divide by 3.6. Or divide by 4 and add a tenth of the result: 100 km/h → 25 + 2.5 = 27.5 m/s, exact 27.8. This is the conversion that matters in physics problems, where a <strong>speedometer online test</strong> result is often the input.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Knots are the odd one: 1 knot is 1.852 km/h, so roughly double the km/h figure and subtract a tenth. 100 km/h → 200 − 20 = 180, and the exact value is 54 knots in the other direction — so for knots, halve the km/h and knock off a tenth instead.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Rather than doing any of this, just switch the tool. The point of a <strong>free online speedometer</strong> with a unit button is that mental arithmetic should never be the bottleneck — and it is why an <strong>online speedometer mph</strong> request is one tap away from a km/h reading.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 3: Compare with a dash in another unit
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">This is where most confusion starts. A car imported from Japan reads km/h, the driver thinks in mph, and the phone can show either — the same mismatch people hit after a <strong>speedometer test online</strong> on a borrowed vehicle, or at the end of a <strong>HardSpeedometer test</strong> on a track day when the results are quoted in km/h. Before you conclude that the tool is wrong, check both numbers are in the same unit.
    </p>

    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>Set the tool to the unit on the vehicle dial.</li>
      <li>Hold a steady speed on a straight, open road.</li>
      <li>Note both numbers at the same moment — not one now and one after a bend.</li>
      <li>Expect the dial to read a few percent high; that is normal and legal.</li>
      <li>If the gap is much bigger than 5%, check the tyre size before doubting either device.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">A <strong>speedometer online mph</strong> reading compared against a km/h dial will look like a catastrophic failure — 62 against 100 — when nothing is wrong at all. Units first, then judgement, and only then an <strong>online speedometer test</strong> of the vehicle itself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Worked example: a hire car in the wrong unit
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">You land in the UK, hire a car, and the dial is in mph. Your phone is still set to km/h from the flight. At 60 on the dial the phone says 97, and you assume the GPS is broken.
    </p>

    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      <li>Switch the tool to mph — the reading becomes 60, matching the dial.</li>
      <li>The 97 was correct all along: 60 mph is 96.6 km/h.</li>
      <li>Note the mental conversion (mph × 1.6 ≈ km/h) so the next glance is quick.</li>
      <li>Check the dash optimism once: at 70 mph the phone should read about 66–69.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Nothing was broken except the unit setting. That is the whole reason the button exists, and why a <strong>gps speedometer online</strong> that hides it behind a menu is a worse tool than one that shows it. The same trap catches anyone doing a <strong>live speed test</strong> on a <strong>speedometer online train</strong> ride in a country that uses a different unit.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Troubleshooting
    </h2>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead><tr class="bg-canvas-elevated"><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Problem</th><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Likely cause</th><th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Fix</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Reading looks 1.6× too high</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Tool in km/h, dial in mph</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Switch the unit button to mph</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Number seems tiny</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Tool in m/s</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Switch back to km/h or mph</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Average and current disagree wildly</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Average carried over from an earlier trip</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Press Reset before the run</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Unit resets on reload</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">No stored profile on the device</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Set the unit again; nothing is saved by design</td></tr><tr><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Boat or aircraft figure looks wrong</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Knots vs km/h confusion</td><td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Choose knots — 1 kn = 1.852 km/h</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      The short version
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">Set the unit before you move, remember that km/h to mph is roughly ×0.62 and km/h to m/s is ÷3.6, and always compare like with like before blaming the tool. A <strong>speedometer online</strong> with a unit button removes the arithmetic entirely — open the <strong>online speedometer</strong>, pick your unit, and the <strong>my speed km/h</strong> question or the mph one is answered in the same tab. It is <strong>online speedometer free</strong> of charge, the readout is a clean <strong>speedometer digital</strong> figure rather than a needle, and the unit is never guessed for you.
    </p>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
